 const chapterHTML=common?`<section class="solver-guide" aria-label="Puzzle Atlas solver handbook">
 <details class="solver-guide-usage"><summary>Using the Constrained Solver</summary><div class="step-body">
<p><strong>Hold a selection, then exchange a pair.</strong> Turn rings is the ordinary playing mode. Switch to <strong>Constrained Solver</strong> for a deliberate, verified operation:</p>
<ol><li>Choose <strong>Select held</strong>. Click a ring-map node, click its tile in the 3D view, or draw a lasso on the map: each selected node flips between held and unheld, including a mixed group. <strong>Suggest holds</strong> replaces your holds with the nodes already matching the solved target and switches to choosing endpoint 1.</li><li>Choose <strong>1</strong> and click the first endpoint in either view; choose <strong>2</strong> and click its partner. A held node may be an endpoint without losing its held classification.</li><li>Inspect the proposed exchange. <strong>Go</strong> runs a verified sequence already in hand, or first searches when one is not ready. Its label is always Go; the result below explains any unsuccessful check.</li><li>Entering Constrained Solver selects <strong>All labels</strong>, ½× speed and visible highlights so you can see held tiles and numbered arrows while choosing the pair. Go selects All labels again if you changed the mode. You can change labels, highlights or speed while it runs. When the whole operation finishes, the pair clears and held locations remain selected. Manual mode keeps your current display settings; leaving it restores <strong>Some labels</strong>, 1× speed and the puzzle’s usual highlights.</li></ol>
<p>Nothing moves merely because you selected two nodes. Colors at active holds are restored after the complete operation, rather than after every individual turn.</p>
<details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="handbook-choose"><summary>Selecting, replacing, and understanding availability</summary><div class="solver-tech-body"><p>Clicking 1 or 2 leaves the Select held tool; click Select held to resume editing holds. Availability is checked while you choose holds. Click the <strong>1</strong> card, then a node; the map immediately uses available results to show which nodes can be <strong>2</strong>. Click the 1 card again to clear both endpoints and choose a new pair, keeping your holds. Click 2 to replace only 2. Changing holds rechecks the pair. </p><p>Bright dots have a stored, verified move sequence ready to run. Moderately dim dots marked <strong>?</strong> remain selectable but need a further check; a mathematical feasibility proof alone does not make a sequence ready. Strongly dim dots are proved unavailable and cannot be selected. First-endpoint choices depend only on the current holds and legal puzzle moves; second-endpoint choices also depend on endpoint 1. With no holds, fixed centers and groups that can only cycle in threes may still have no legal pair exchange. </p><p>After choosing 1 and 2, <strong>Go</strong> plays the stored verified sequence for a ready pair. For a pair without a ready sequence, <strong>Go</strong> first begins a search; success is not guaranteed. Manual mode begins with All labels and highlights on, which selects ½× animation. Go selects All labels if necessary; you can adjust the speed or labels during the operation. Finishing a swap keeps manual mode and its current settings. Leaving Constrained Solver restores Some labels and 1×. The move-progress bar shows completed turns. After playback, 1 and 2 clear automatically; only held nodes remain selected, ready for a new pair.</p><p><strong>Preserve what is correct.</strong> <strong>Suggest holds</strong> releases your current holds and selects every occupied node whose current color matches its defined solved color. It clears the old 1/2 pair and any unfinished search, then switches from editing holds to choosing endpoint 1. A same-colored replacement counts as correct; sticker identity is not required. A manually chosen arbitrary pair may still be impossible or unresolved. Selected endpoints still temporarily override their own holds.</p><p><strong>See the target.</strong> In either Turn rings or Constrained Solver, press and hold <strong>Show goal</strong> to preview solved colors at the current ring-map locations; release to see the current colors again. Space or Enter works while the button has keyboard focus. The puzzle, camera, journal and selections do not change. This uses the solver’s defined home orientation, rather than choosing a different uniform-color arrangement. In shape-changing puzzles, a hollow gray node means that pose has no occupied sticker in the solved shape; it is not assigned an invented goal color. Preview is available while the puzzle is still, and node selection is suspended until you release it. Show goal is hidden in mirror mode, where the ring-map nodes are silver instead of displaying the hidden color scheme.</p></div></details><details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="handbook-hold-semantics"><summary>What a hold means, including held endpoints</summary><div class="solver-tech-body"><p>A hold protects the <strong>color at that location</strong> after the <em>whole operation</em>. A held node can also be selected as 1 or 2: it keeps its dashed held marker, but is temporarily exempt from color protection while it is an endpoint. Changing the endpoint restores its hold immediately. After a completed exchange, the original held locations remain held with their new colors; 1 and 2 clear. Another sticker of the same color may replace the original sticker. Intermediate turns can change the held location temporarily. The selected 1/2 pair still exchanges its two sticker identities, even if its colors match; other nodes may change. This is not necessarily an isolated two-sticker transposition.</p></div></details><details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="handbook-continuation"><summary>Search progress, continuation, and limits</summary><div class="solver-tech-body"><p><strong>Continue makes new progress.</strong> An unfinished search keeps its explored states and pending work. The first check has a 3-second planning budget; <strong>Continue previous pair · +7s</strong>, then <strong>+20s</strong> and <strong>+30s</strong>, raise the cumulative budget to 10, 30, then 60 seconds. Elapsed planning time and the explored-state count show real progress; the search bar does not pretend to know how much of the unknown search remains. An unsuccessful check clears both endpoint selections and leaves holds unchanged. Continue previous pair explicitly restores those two endpoints and resumes the saved search instead of starting over. The puzzle stays unchanged until a complete sequence has been found and independently verified. If the search reaches its memory, state, output, or final time limit, the continue action disappears and the result explains that no option was found within the limits. This is not proof that the swap is impossible. Try another pair or release some holds. Changing holds, 1/2, the puzzle state, or mode discards that selection’s unfinished search.</p><p><strong>Impossible</strong> means the legal-move model proves the request cannot be done. <strong>Unresolved</strong> means a computation or output limit was reached; it does not mean impossible. Neither result moves the puzzle. Cancel is available during search. During a manual swap, Stop finishes the current primitive turn and discards the unplayed remainder. During Auto Solve, the first Stop finishes the current complete step; its caption becomes <strong>Stop now</strong>, and clicking again finishes only the current primitive turn. The held colors and selected exchange are guaranteed only if the whole sequence completes; stopping early can leave them changed. The result remains a legal puzzle state, and the journal can undo its completed moves.</p></div></details><details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="handbook-selection-visuals"><summary>Reading the selections in both views</summary><div class="solver-tech-body"><p><strong>A dashed white circle means held.</strong> The active rotation ring uses the usual solid white node highlight; a node that is also held has a dashed white circle outside it. In ordinary Turn rings mode, selecting an axis highlights its moving nodes. In Constrained Solver, only the axis of an executing move is highlighted. Subtle gray circles and gray 1/2 labels mark the original locations behind the moving rings and dots; these stay fixed during each manual or automatic step.</p><p><strong>Select from the surface.</strong> In Constrained Solver, a stationary click or tap on a visible 3D tile applies the current Select held, 1, or 2 tool exactly as clicking its ring-map node. Dragging still rotates the camera, pinching still zooms, and axis badges retain priority over the underlying tile. Selection is disabled during a move or goal preview. In Turn rings, hovering over a 3D tile adds a bold white outline to its corresponding ring-map node; it clears when you leave the tile or panel, begin dragging, or start any animation. Clicking a tile in Turn rings cycles through its applicable axes, one at a time, followed by no selection. Homer uses its exact visible surface and logical surface regions, including the sculpted details.</p><p><strong>Read the same selections in 3D.</strong> White dashed tile borders follow held stickers; solid white borders mark the active turn. With <strong>All labels</strong>, the numbered 1 and 2 arrows follow the selected stickers and stay perpendicular to their faces, including during Auto Solve. Their short stems end just before the number badges. They remain visible from any viewing direction wherever the puzzle does not physically cover them; only the occluded portions disappear. The 3D label control cycles through <strong>Some labels</strong> (the default: on-surface axes only), <strong>All labels</strong> (supported extra axes, leader lines and 1/2 arrows), and <strong>No labels</strong>. Its text names the next action: Some labels appears when labels are off, All labels appears in the default Some mode, and No labels appears when All is active. Its hover tip also identifies the current mode. Some and No both hide the 1/2 arrows. All includes the supported labels; inner slices are identified when selected rather than labeled permanently. Changing the label mode sets the corresponding playback rate: No = 2×, Some = 1×, All = ½×. A separate speed adjustment overrides that rate until the next label-mode change. There is no tile-label control. <strong>Hide highlights</strong> switches the 3D tile outlines off without changing any selection. Highlights default on except for Homer, where they default off. Entering manual Constrained Solver turns highlights on even for Homer; leaving restores the puzzle’s default. The ring map retains its active-ring highlight. In Turn rings mode, the ring map has its own <strong>Hide labels / Show labels</strong> control beside the ring-focus control. Click an axis badge in either view to select the corresponding ring. Outer-axis labels orient both views; selecting or hovering over a ring also identifies that axis in 3D, including inner slices. No ring is selected when a puzzle opens. Click empty dark space in the ring panel to deselect its ring. Switching modes, completing Randomize, and completing Auto Solve clear the previous ring highlight. Solver playback highlights each executing move, then clears the highlight when the sequence finishes. With no ring selected, the control panel below the 3D view prompts <strong>Select axis</strong>.</p></div></details><details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="handbook-auto"><summary>Auto Solve, stopping, and diagnostics</summary><div class="solver-tech-body"><p><strong>Auto Solve</strong> reads the current arrangement, opens the ring map, and secures a growing set of solved nodes. Auto Solve begins with No labels, which sets 2× speed. Choose All labels during playback to follow the numbered arrows at ½×, or Some labels for the on-surface axes at 1×. The speed control can override either rate. Each completed step is followed by a short pause. The upper progress bar counts nodes secured by completed solving steps. It grows as the solver preserves its earlier holds and adds newly solved locations. The lower bar counts completed moves within the current step. Steps retain the solver’s smaller placement boundaries, with a short pause after each one; a placement or cycle need not be a two-node swap. Only the displayed secured nodes are protected at the next endpoint. Other colors that happen to match the goal may move again. Exact shape or picture goals require their correct home poses; their same-colored tiles are not automatically treated as solved. Numbered swap arrows appear in the 3D view only with All labels, including during Auto Solve.</p><p>The first click on the top <strong>Stop</strong> button requests a graceful stop after the current complete step, preserving its endpoint guarantee. The caption changes to <strong>Stop now</strong>; click again to finish only the current primitive turn and discard the remaining sequence. After an early stop, held and numbered selections clear because their complete-step guarantees may not have been restored. Stop cancels planning before any move begins. Stopping or completing Auto Solve returns to ordinary controls with Some labels and 1× speed. Clicking <strong>Constrained Solver</strong> during Auto Solve instead finishes the current complete step and enters manual mode with All labels and highlights on. Hiding the ring map stops playback and exits Constrained Solver; stopping partway through an operation may leave held nodes temporarily displaced. The puzzle remains in a legal state.</p><p>Auto Solve shows its preparation status near the top of the page immediately: loading the move model, loading its solution table, planning, then verifying. If preparation fails, the reason stays visible with recovery controls. A release mismatch offers <strong>Reload puzzle</strong>; an already-open tab can otherwise retain older scripts after a deployment. Download, browser-worker, data-verification, memory, and computation failures are reported separately. Stop also cancels loading. Retry gives planning more time; it cannot make an illegal state solvable.</p></div></details><p class="solver-guide-note">Keyboard: focus the map, use arrow keys to choose a node, then Enter or Space to apply the current tool. Choose <strong>Turn rings</strong> to return to ordinary ring dragging. During Auto Solve this finishes the current complete step before returning; during manual constrained playback it finishes only the current primitive turn. Randomize, reset, and completed Auto Solve return to Turn rings; reopening a hidden ring map also starts there.</p></div></details>
 <details class="solver-guide-algorithm"><summary>General approach &amp; algorithm</summary><div class="step-body">
 <p>Each legal turn is a permutation of node addresses. We compose legal turns into small operations that preserve previous progress, then use increasingly restrictive operations to finish the puzzle. A <em>stabilizer chain</em> makes this principle exact. ${cite('sims1971')}</p>
 <p>There are three related tasks: find a short manual swap while preserving held colors; determine which pairs are available; and restore the puzzle with Auto Solve. They share permutation algebra but use different computational paths. The original identity-preserving stabilizer solver remains a reliable fallback. Small legal-move catalogs and bounded color-aware searches allow more freedom without enumerating all configurations. Holds are endpoint constraints, so intermediate turns may disturb them.</p>
 <details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="tech-overview"><summary>Overview: the mathematical outline</summary><div class="solver-tech-body">
 <h4>1. Define the legal transformations</h4>
 <dl class="solver-terms"><div><dt><i>N</i></dt><dd>Number of node addresses in the model, including geometric pose addresses when needed.</dd></div><div><dt><i>m</i></dt><dd>Number of available primitive move permutations.</dd></div><div><dt><i>P</i><sub>j</sub></dt><dd>The <i>j</i>th legal move, a bijection of the <i>N</i> addresses. Its matrix form is <i>N</i> × <i>N</i>; the implementation stores an array of <i>N</i> destinations.</dd></div><div><dt><i>G</i></dt><dd>All transformations generated by the legal moves.</dd></div><div><dt><i>F</i>, <i>a</i>, <i>b</i></dt><dd>The active held addresses and the two distinct occupied addresses selected for exchange. An endpoint is temporarily excluded from the active held set even if its node remains marked held in the interface.</dd></div></dl>
 ${math('G is the group generated by P one through P m, a subgroup of S N','<mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow>')}
 <p><i>S</i><sub>N</sub> denotes all permutations of <i>N</i> addresses. The puzzle usually permits only a small subgroup. Pieces, orientation restrictions, parity, and immovable addresses are encoded by the move permutations themselves.</p>
 <h4>2. Express a held-color request</h4>
 <p>Let <i>c</i>(<i>x</i>) be the initial color at address <i>x</i>, and let <i>g</i> send starting addresses to final addresses. The color arriving at held address <i>f</i> comes from <i>g</i><sup>−1</sup>(<i>f</i>):</p>
 ${math('Preserve each held color and exchange the two selected stickers','<mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo><mspace width=".5em"/><mo>∀</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=".5em"/><mi>g</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow>')}
 <p>This includes every old solution fixing the held stickers, and can include additional legal solutions. Selected-location color preservation is generally not a subgroup, so it cannot simply replace the pointwise stabilizer in Schreier–Sims. The balanced method checks short precomputed legal operations, tries a bounded cached strict transporter, and searches exact color-preimage tuples. The browser retains the tuple search between continuation clicks, within fixed time and memory limits. A strict solution is accepted; a strict impossibility is <em>not</em> a proof that the relaxed request is impossible.</p>
 <p><strong>Which swap partners are dimmed?</strong> Full-group ordered-pair orbits give valid negative proofs even without holds. Strict held-stabilizer results give valid positive proofs under color holds. Short legal operations can establish additional positives. Those proofs describe mathematical feasibility. A separate readiness layer makes a choice bright only when a verified sequence is already stored. Feasible-but-not-ready and unchecked choices are moderately dim, marked <strong>?</strong>, and selectable through <strong>Go</strong>, which checks the request before playback. Only proved-impossible choices are disabled. Colors at all addresses belong in cache keys because an unheld same-color sticker may supply a held location.</p>
 <p>The worker stores pair classifications in <i>O</i>(<i>N</i><sup>2</sup>) bits and a bounded number of short tuples, not a table of full puzzle configurations. This is a representation bound, not a guarantee that every color request finishes within the time limit.</p>
 <h4>3. Precompute a stabilizer chain</h4>
 <p>Choose an ordered <em>base</em> of addresses <i>b</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>b</i><sub>r</sub> whose pointwise stabilizer is the identity. Let <i>G</i><sub>0</sub> = <i>G</i> and successively fix the next base address:</p>
 ${math('G zero contains G one through G r, where G r is the identity','<mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow>')}
 ${math('G i equals elements h of G i minus one which fix b i','<mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow>')}
 <p>At level <i>i</i>, compute the addresses reachable from <i>b</i><sub>i</sub> while fixing earlier base addresses. For each reachable address <i>q</i>, store one representative <i>t</i><sub>i,q</sub> with <i>t</i><sub>i,q</sub>(<i>b</i><sub>i</sub>) = <i>q</i>, together with its expression in legal moves.</p>
 <p>Let <i>P</i> be a generator of <i>G</i><sub>i−1</sub> and <i>q</i> an address in the current orbit. The representative <i>t</i><sub>i,q</sub> sends the base point to <i>q</i>; applying <i>P</i> sends it to <i>P</i>(<i>q</i>). Undo that destination’s representative to obtain a <em>Schreier generator</em> that fixes the base point:</p>
 ${math('s equals inverse t i P of q, times P, times t i q; s fixes b i','<mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=".6em"/><mi>s</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow>')}
 <p>Products here apply the rightmost transformation first, as with column vectors. <em>Sifting</em> removes a candidate’s action on successive base points using the stored representatives. A remaining nonidentity action supplies a missing deeper generator; repeat until the chain is complete. A <em>strong generating set</em> provides generators for every subgroup in the chain. ${cite('sims1971')}</p>
 <p>Precomputation uses those strong generators; the browser’s reusable full-solve cache stores the orbit representatives and a shared expression graph for their legal moves. Expressions reuse existing graph references; this is not a global deduplication of all equivalent subexpressions. This represents the needed operations without listing every configuration. Compact group representation and constructive factorization are established tools. ${cite('jerrum1986')}</p>
 <h4>4. Restore the current state, one level at a time</h4>
 <p>Suppose all earlier base addresses are secured and the sticker belonging at <i>b</i><sub>i</sub> is currently at <i>q</i>. Apply the inverse representative:</p>
 ${math('g i equals the inverse of t i q; g i of q equals b i','<mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=".6em"/><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow>')}
 <p>Because <i>g</i><sub>i</sub> belongs to <i>G</i><sub>i−1</sub>, it restores all earlier secured addresses. Add the newly restored address to the secured prefix and continue. If subsequent addresses are already correct, they can be secured without an extra move. Auto Solve constructs this identity-correct baseline from current stickers and their home addresses, without the move journal. For supported fixed-geometry color puzzles, a bounded second pass removes repeated visible-color states and stops at the earliest solved color state on the resulting route. It verifies and rebuilds stage boundaries before playback. Shape and picture puzzles retain their stricter goal. Auto Solve keeps these verified incremental boundaries and secures their declared solved locations. It does not merge them merely to protect incidental correct colors elsewhere on the puzzle. The secured prefix grows after each complete step; the actual goal-match total can rise or fall outside that prefix.</p>
 <h4>5. Keep expressions short, then verify</h4>
 <p>Some precomputations choose representatives by the current generator-word cost; others use unweighted orbit search, including the 7×7×7 and 8×8×8 caches. Neither policy guarantees shortest primitive words. Reusable short operations, cancellations, and equivalent move expressions can reduce playback further. Short generator words are a separate optimization problem from constructing a correct chain; they are not automatically shortest. ${cite('minkwitz1998')}</p>
 <p>Before playback, compose the proposed legal moves and check both requested sticker destinations and the incoming color at every held address. Auto Solve additionally verifies its stated identity or color goal and every secured stage boundary. Check the live state again at each completed step. A stale or failed verification prevents further playback. Only a complete step has its endpoint guarantee.</p>
 <h4>What is exact, and what is optimal?</h4>
 <p>A verified result exactly meets its declared sticker or color constraints. A completed chain gives a constructive route for states in its legal group. The browser still applies time, memory, and output limits, so a legal state can remain unresolved in a particular attempt. Auto Solve does not prove a globally shortest solution. A manual request is labeled shortest only when its search proves that claim.</p>
 <p>The implementation caches reusable move expressions and loads only the current puzzle’s model. It does not store all configurations or all held subsets. Shape-changing models also track occupied geometric poses. Advanced Helicopter jumbling has state-dependent cuts and is outside this fixed-permutation solver.</p>
 </div></details>
 ${window.SOLVER_TECHNICAL.render({cite})}
 </div></details>
 <details class="solver-panel-guide" id="how-to-play"><summary>How to play — illustrated controls</summary><div class="step-body">
 <p>Choose a puzzle, then select an axis and turn it from either panel. The ring map and 3D view show the same puzzle and share one selected axis. Drag a ring directly, or use the signed rotation buttons below the 3D view. Use the guide beside each game for that puzzle’s solution; this chapter explains the shared controls.</p>
 <details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="help-axis-families"><summary>Axis families, layers, and rings</summary><div class="solver-tech-body">
 <p>The atlas counts <strong>turning directions from the center</strong>, also called half-axes or turning poles. Opposite ends of one geometric axis count separately. This follows the puzzle-classification convention used by Jaap Scherphuis; the category describes the mechanism, not necessarily the outer shape. ${citeWeb({web:true,author:'J. Scherphuis',title:'Ideas for further puzzles',label:'Jaap Scherphuis · Ideas for further puzzles',site:'Jaap’s Puzzle Page',url:'https://www.jaapsch.net/puzzles/ideas.htm',accessed:'Oct. 15, 2026'})}</p>
 <table><thead><tr><th>Atlas family</th><th>Turning poles</th><th>Geometric axis lines</th><th>Typical direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tetrahedral</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>Tetrahedron vertices or face normals</td></tr><tr><td>Cubical</td><td>6</td><td>3</td><td>Cube faces / octahedron vertices</td></tr><tr><td>Octahedral</td><td>8</td><td>4</td><td>Cube corners / octahedron faces</td></tr><tr><td>Dodecahedral</td><td>12</td><td>6</td><td>Dodecahedron faces, or cube edges</td></tr></tbody></table>
 <p>The tetrahedral directions have no opposite partner in their set. The 12-pole category deliberately combines two different arrangements: regular dodecahedral face turns, and Helicopter Cube edge turns associated with a rhombic dodecahedron. They are not the same symmetry family. A MasterMorphix stays Cubical despite its tetrahedral shell; a corner-turning octahedron also belongs there. A Skewb uses eight turning sides even though its core has four arms; opposite-side descriptions need not be independent generators.</p>
 <p><strong>More layers do not add geometric axis lines.</strong> Every N×N×N cube here has three perpendicular axis lines and six outer turning poles. With N separately selectable layers about each line, this app provides <strong>3N single-layer controls</strong>: 9 for 3×3×3, 12 for 4×4×4, and 30 for 10×10×10. These are layers, not cutting planes: an aligned N×N×N grid has <strong>3(N−1) internal cutting planes</strong>. Clockwise, inverse, and half turns are different actions on a selected layer, not additional axes. Conventional fixed-center 3×3×3 notation often chooses only the six outer face turns as generators; this app also exposes the middle slices.</p>
 <p><strong>A drawn ring is a motion path, not a category count.</strong> One layer turn can move several separate cycles of nodes and therefore use several rings. Extra face tracks and layout choices also affect how many paths are drawn. More layers usually mean more pieces and work, but neither the axis count nor the drawn-ring count measures difficulty or shortest solution length. Piece orbits, orientation and parity restrictions, move interactions, and the chosen goal all matter.</p>
 </div></details>
 <details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="help-turns"><summary>Read the ring map and make a turn</summary><div class="solver-tech-body">
 <div class="help-figure-grid"><figure class="help-capture"><img src="assets/help-turn-rings.png" alt="Cube 3×3×3 ring map with the U axis selected; callout A points to its solid white moving-node outlines." width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><strong>A · Active turn.</strong> Bright rings trace the selected move. Solid white outlines show the nodes that move.</figcaption></figure><figure class="help-capture"><img src="assets/help-turn-3d.png" alt="The same Cube 3×3×3 with the upper layer’s physical tiles outlined in solid white and its U axis highlighted." width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><strong>The same U turn in 3D.</strong> Matching solid borders identify the moving tiles.</figcaption></figure></div>
 <p class="help-capture-note">Illustrations captured from the game’s actual canvas renderer. The lettered callouts are annotations; these are play-surface captures, not screenshots of the surrounding browser controls.</p>
 <ol><li>Choose <strong>Turn rings</strong>. Select an axis using the selector below the 3D view, a ring, an axis badge, or a tile. Repeated tile clicks cycle through its applicable axes; after the last axis, another click clears the selection. Click empty ring-map space to deselect it. Both panels always share one selection.</li><li>In the ring map, grab and drag along a ring to turn it. The drag follows the drawn path; it does not depend on the 3D camera. The ring map has no separate axis-selector or rotation-button panel.</li><li>In 3D, use <strong>Rotate ↶</strong> for a positive right-hand rotation or <strong>Rotate ↷</strong> for its negative inverse. Look from the selected axis’s outer end toward the puzzle center to read these directions. The right thumb points outward along the selected stem; curled fingers indicate positive rotation. Looking from the opposite side reverses its apparent screen direction, without changing the command. Ring dragging follows the flattened diagram rather than the 3D camera’s viewpoint.</li><li>The 3D rotation buttons are disabled when no axis is selected. The center says <strong>Select axis</strong> until an axis is chosen; “selected” appears only for an active axis. A cube face advances 90°, an outer Megaminx face 72°, and a main Pyraminx turn 120°. Use each puzzle’s guide for other move angles.</li></ol>
 <p><strong>Follow each applied move.</strong> During Auto Solve or a constrained-solver sequence, the active axis button and the matching <strong>Rotate ↶</strong> or <strong>Rotate ↷</strong> button flash together for each primitive turn. The direction is the physical 3D direction described above, including for 180° moves whose two directions reach the same final arrangement. Repeated turns trigger a fresh cue. The cue clears between steps and after playback; disabled controls remain unavailable while a sequence is running. Reduced-motion settings retain the paired highlight without a flashing animation.</p>
 <p><strong>Inner layers have a fixed viewing side.</strong> An indexed cube slice, such as Ri, uses the same outward direction as its named face R. For the central slices, <strong>M uses L, E uses D, and S uses F</strong>. These are direction conventions, not additional physical axes. The 3D controls translate these directions into the existing legal moves; the ring paths, guide notation, journal, and solver permutations are unchanged. Ordinary unprimed face notation is CW and its primed inverse is CCW. For a 180° move, the two signs have the same final arrangement but opposite animation paths.</p>
 <p>Face groups collect the colors on each physical face; they are not additional moves. Drag the 3D model to look around; scroll or pinch to zoom. Hovering over a visible tile temporarily outlines its ring-map node. This outline clears when the pointer leaves, while dragging, or whenever animation starts. Hover over a control to read its action without adding permanent instructions to the play area. The 3D header keeps its settings on one line with single-line button captions. If optional extra controls exceed a narrow screen’s width, swipe the header sideways to reach them.</p>
 </div></details>
 <details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="help-notation"><summary>Follow named moves in a solution</summary><div class="solver-tech-body">
 <p>Some specialized guides use exact mechanism slots. On those puzzles, in colored <strong>Turn rings</strong> mode, hover over a ring-map node or tap it to read its temporary <strong>piece reference</strong>: home → current slot, plus its orientation. This is a tooltip, not a label drawn over the puzzle. It disappears when you leave the node, drag, scroll or start a move; it is hidden during solver operations and in Mirror mode. The guide defines the fixed slot names used by its move builders.</p>
 <p>A guide’s unprimed move and its <strong>primed inverse</strong>, for example R and R′, turn the same axis in opposite directions. For ordinary face turns, R is clockwise when looking directly at the named R face from outside the puzzle, and R′ reverses it. An inner-slice or corner-turn label follows that guide’s defined axis instead of assuming a visible square face. A half-turn move can be its own inverse.</p>
 <p>Select the named axis below the 3D panel, then use <strong>Rotate ↶</strong> or <strong>Rotate ↷</strong>. Each arrow’s tooltip names the corresponding notation move. The ring’s screen direction can differ from a guide’s clockwise direction. Looking along the same physical axis from its opposite end reverses the apparent clockwise sense; orbiting the camera does not reverse the move buttons. If unsure on a touch device, make one turn, read its name in the Move journal, then undo that turn before continuing. Read sequences from left to right, including every prime. A notation suffix 2 means a double turn where that puzzle’s guide defines it.</p>
 <p>In Constrained Solver, the manual move buttons are disabled so selecting holds or tiles cannot accidentally turn the puzzle. Dragging to orbit and scrolling or pinching to zoom remain available. Keyboard move shortcuts are listed in each puzzle’s Move journal help.</p>
 </div></details>
 <details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="help-held"><summary>Hold colors and exchange tiles 1 and 2</summary><div class="solver-tech-body">
 <div class="help-figure-grid"><figure class="help-capture"><img src="assets/help-hold-rings.png" alt="Constrained Solver ring map; callout B identifies a dashed held node, and thick solid circles identify selected tiles 1 and 2. Question marks indicate pairs not yet verified." width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><strong>B · Held color.</strong> A dashed circle protects a location’s color at the completed endpoint. Thick solid circles and numbers identify 1 and 2.</figcaption></figure><figure class="help-capture"><img src="assets/help-hold-3d.png" alt="Matching 3D cube with dashed white borders around held tiles and outward arrows numbered 1 and 2 on the selected tiles." width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><strong>Linked surface selection with All labels.</strong> Dashed borders and numbered arrows connect the same selections to the physical puzzle.</figcaption></figure></div>
 <p>Switch to <strong>Constrained Solver</strong>; All labels and highlights turn on for selecting tiles, while manual 3D move buttons are disabled. Use <strong>Select held</strong> to toggle nodes individually, with a lasso, or by clicking visible 3D tiles. <strong>Suggest holds</strong> selects current goal matches and switches directly to choosing endpoint 1. Choose the <strong>1</strong> card and a tile, then <strong>2</strong> and its partner. A held endpoint stays marked held but is exempt from protection for this exchange. <strong>Go</strong> runs a verified sequence, first searching if needed. No moves start merely from selecting a pair.</p>
 <p>The request really exchanges the two selected sticker identities and restores all other active held colors at the endpoint. Other unheld tiles may move. Another sticker of the same color may supply a held location. Intermediate turns may disturb held colors. The solver tries short cached operations and bounded searches, but a verified sequence is not necessarily shortest.</p>
 <p><strong>Stop</strong> ends a manual operation after its current primitive turn, without waiting for the entire word. At this partial endpoint, the swap or held colors may not yet be restored. Use the journal to reverse the completed moves if desired. The full <a href="#handbook-choose">Constrained Solver instructions</a> explain availability, replacing endpoints, and continuing a search.</p>
 </div></details>
 <details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="help-toolbar"><summary>Top controls, playback speed, and the move journal</summary><div class="solver-tech-body">
 <p>The top controls run in this order: <strong>Randomize</strong>, <strong>Auto Solve</strong>, the <strong>journal player</strong>, <strong>playback speed</strong>, <strong>Stop</strong>, and <strong>Reset</strong>. On narrow screens they wrap into compact rows. The controls have their own space rather than covering the puzzle.</p>
 <dl class="help-control-list"><div><dt>Randomize</dt><dd>Applies new legal moves to the current position; it does not reset first. Each sequence records its <strong>Randomize seed</strong> in the move journal and in copied moves.</dd></div><div><dt>Auto Solve</dt><dd>Plans from the actual current state, opens the ring map, and plays a verified solution. The upper progress bar shows secured nodes; the lower bar shows turns completed in the current sequence. Long sequences are possible. This solver does not certify globally shortest solutions.</dd></div><div><dt>Journal player</dt><dd>The inner backward/forward buttons undo or redo one completed move. The outer skip buttons undo or redo the remaining part of the adjacent named sequence. Hover labels name each action. The activity readout identifies the sequence being replayed.</dd></div><div><dt>Playback speed</dt><dd>Choose a playback rate: ½× or ¾× is slower, 1× is normal, and 1½× or 2× is faster. This changes animation only, not the legal move or the search budget. Changing the 3D label mode also sets a rate: No labels = 2×, Some labels = 1×, All labels = ½×. You can then choose a different rate without changing the labels.</dd></div><div><dt>Stop</dt><dd>Stops the active operation according to its rules below. It stays disabled when there is nothing to stop.</dd></div><div><dt>Reset</dt><dd>Restores the puzzle to its solved arrangement and clears the move journal.</dd></div></dl>
 <p>Journal playback is locked during Randomize and solver operations. Stop during journal replay cancels an unfinished visual turn and keeps the last completed legal turn; the remaining history stays available. You can resume forward or backward through the same sequence. A new move from inside a sequence discards its future branch and names the retained prefix <strong>· split</strong>. Replaying history adds no new journal entries. The journal player, including both skip buttons, preserves your chosen labels and playback speed.</p>
 <p>Randomize and Auto Solve switch to No labels, which selects 2×. Entering manual Constrained Solver selects All labels, ½× speed and highlights on, including for Homer. You can change these settings while selecting or solving. Manual playback keeps its current settings when it finishes or stops, ready for the next pair. Exiting manual mode, ending Randomize or returning from Auto Solve restores Some labels, 1× speed and the usual display defaults: visible ring labels and 3D highlights on except Homer’s default off. Brief accents identify actual setting changes; temporary disabled/enabled states and changing progress captions do not pulse.</p>
 </div></details>
 <details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="help-display"><summary>Panels, labels, mirror mode, and goal preview</summary><div class="solver-tech-body">
 <p>The ring map opens on the left. Hide it with its left-pointing arrow and reopen it from 3D with the right-pointing arrow. The solution guide opens on the right and uses the opposite arrows. Each panel scrolls independently where needed. The guide contains the puzzle-specific solution; shared explanations stay in this handbook.</p>
 <p>The compact 3D header keeps the panel title, label and highlight settings, and panel controls on one line. The axis selector and Rotate ↶ / Rotate ↷ buttons sit below the 3D view. The ring map remains directly draggable, without a separate control panel. Drag the model to orbit the camera; scroll or pinch to zoom. Settings occupy their own space rather than covering the puzzle.</p>
 <table><thead><tr><th>3D label mode</th><th>Visible labels</th><th>Rate selected</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>No labels</strong></td><td>No axes or 1/2 arrows</td><td>2×</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Some labels</strong> · default</td><td>On-surface axes, without leader lines or 1/2 arrows</td><td>1×</td></tr><tr><td><strong>All labels</strong></td><td>Supported axes and leader lines, plus 1/2 arrows when a pair is selected</td><td>½×</td></tr></tbody></table>
 <p>The button names the next action, not the current mode. From the default Some mode it reads All labels; clicking changes to All and the button becomes No labels. Clicking again hides the labels and changes the button to Some labels. The hover tip identifies the current mode and the next action. Choosing a label mode sets its rate; adjusting playback speed afterward leaves the labels unchanged. Axis badges in either view select the same ring. Leader lines, arrows and number badges are occluded only where the puzzle physically covers them. The ring map has an independent <strong>Hide labels / Show labels</strong> button. <strong>Show all rings</strong> reveals the complete paths; the alternative emphasizes the selected ones. <strong>Hide highlights</strong> affects 3D borders, not the current selection. Labels and highlights do not alter the puzzle state.</p>
 <p>Eligible irregular puzzles offer <strong>Mirror mode</strong> on the left of the header’s settings buttons; Homer does not. The button receives one brief accent on opening. Silver tiles and silver ring-map nodes conceal colors, while selection, held markers and ring paths remain available. Mirror mode restores the display defaults and hides <strong>Show goal</strong>. Reduced-motion preferences suppress scaling animation.</p>
 <p>In either playing mode, press and hold <strong>Show goal</strong> to preview solved node colors, then release to return to the current colors. Space or Enter works when the button has keyboard focus. It does not move tiles or change the journal. Hollow nodes on a shape-changing map mean an unoccupied pose in the solved shape. Preview is unavailable during animation or in mirror mode.</p>
 </div></details>
 <details class="solver-tech-subsection" id="help-progress"><summary>Solver progress and stopping</summary><div class="solver-tech-body">
 <p>During <strong>Auto Solve</strong>, the upper progress bar measures <strong>secured nodes divided by total physical nodes</strong>. Its secured count grows only after the solver verifies a complete step; it stays fixed while that step is being animated. The lower bar tracks individual moves in the current step. The secured count is shown once, above the upper bar; the panel keeps its space through the pauses. These bars describe the running plan, not a distance in moves to the solved state.</p>
 <p>The manual constrained progress row reserves its space before Go so starting playback does not resize the panel. A completed manual swap guarantees its requested exchange and held colors; an arbitrary swap need not bring the puzzle closer to solved. For ordinary play, compare the visible arrangement with <strong>Show goal</strong>, or use the puzzle’s solution guide. The move journal keeps the turn history.</p>
 <p>The first <strong>Stop</strong> during Auto Solve waits for the complete current step. Its <strong>Stop now</strong> action interrupts after the current primitive turn, as does Stop during a manual swap. An interrupted partial sequence clears its holds and 1/2 selection and has no checkpoint guarantee. Randomize and journal replay instead stop at the last already committed turn, canceling an unfinished visual turn. Read the operation’s result or diagnostic if playback ends before completion.</p>
 </div></details>
 </div></details>
 </section>`:'';
